## Title: Daseeki Bags
## Notes: A single-window bag interface for WoW Classic Era. Requires Daseeki-Core.
## Author: Daseeki
## Category: Bags & Inventory
## X-License: All Rights Reserved
## X-Curse-Project-ID: 1592410
## Dependencies: Daseeki-Core
## OptionalDeps: Daseeki-Nexus
## Interface: 11507, 11508, 11509
## Version: 2.0.8
## SavedVariables: DaseekiBags2DB, DaseekiBags2Data, DaseekiBagsAccount, DaseekiBagsSets, DaseekiBagsMesh
##
## Daseeki-Bags 2.0 — the clean rewrite, now SHIPPING. This is the one and only
## .toc: the addon folder is still Daseeki-Bags, so the client still loads this
## file, and every 1.x install upgrades in place. The side-by-side beta tocs
## (Daseeki-Bags2.toc, v2.toc) were retired at this cutover, and the 1.x source
## tree (main.xml + core/ config/ frames/ skin/ libs/) was removed with them;
## git history keeps it, and tag v1.1.5 is the last release that loaded it.
##
## IDENTITY IS UNCHANGED ACROSS THE CUTOVER (release-gate C.11): same folder name
## (Daseeki-Bags), same X-Curse-Project-ID (1592410), same SavedVariables FILE
## (WTF/.../SavedVariables/Daseeki-Bags.lua — it is named for the folder), same
## Bindings.xml action names, same slash commands. Nothing here asks the client,
## CurseForge, or the user's WTF folder to treat this as a different addon.
##
## DEPENDENCIES — Daseeki-Core IS HARD, AND HONESTLY SO (release verification N1).
## Until 2.0.0 Daseeki-Core sat on OptionalDeps and the README claimed Bags "works
## standalone". It does not, and the failure was silent and total: ui_frame.lua's
## Frame.Ensure() returns nil the moment _G.DaseekiUI is absent (no window can ever be
## built), while Frame.HookBagToggles() replaced all NINE Blizzard bag globals
## unconditionally. Result on a Core-less install: the bag key opens nothing, the
## Blizzard bags are gone too, and nothing is printed. A hard "## Dependencies" line is
## the honest statement — the client refuses to load Bags without Core rather than
## loading it into a state where it cannot draw. The matching CurseForge relation lives
## in .pkgmeta (required-dependencies: daseeki-core) so the site installs Core with us.
##
## Daseeki-Nexus stays OPTIONAL and must NOT be promoted: nexus.lua is fully type-guarded
## and Bags is unchanged without it. Its presence on OptionalDeps is a LOAD-ORDER
## statement only (Nexus SavedVariables must be attached before our ADDON_LOADED runs
## Store.Init -> Migrate.Migrate, or the mesh-import deferral decides blind).
##
## Defence in depth: even a hard dependency can be force-loaded with the dependency
## disabled, so Frame.OnLogin re-checks _G.DaseekiUI, SKIPS the toggle hook and prints one
## plain line when it is missing. The Blizzard bags then survive. See ui_frame.lua.
##
## SAVEDVARIABLES CONTRACT (ROLLOUT_CONTINUITY_AUDIT AT-RISK-1 — the cutover's
## most destructive defect, and the reason all FIVE globals are declared here):
## the client rewrites this addon's SavedVariables file at every logout from the
## DECLARED names only. Any global not listed above is simply not written back —
## so a 2.0 toc that declared only the 2.0 pair would erase the three 1.x globals
## at the first logout, silently and for good. Migration would get exactly ONE
## attempt and rollback would be impossible.
##
## 2.0 therefore DECLARES the 1.x trio while never writing it: migrate.lua,
## migrate_settings.lua and locks.lua read it READ-ONLY. Declaring it keeps it on
## disk as a rollback net, so a partial or failed import can re-run on a later
## login and the owner can downgrade to 1.x with data intact.
##
## KEEP ALL FIVE DECLARED FOR AT LEAST 2 RELEASES AFTER CUTOVER (through 2.2.0 at
## the earliest). Dropping a 1.x name from this line deletes that data at the very
## next logout.
##   DaseekiBags2DB     — 2.0 settings (schema-versioned)
##   DaseekiBags2Data   — 2.0 inventory cache DB (owners graph; per-container identity)
##   DaseekiBagsAccount — 1.x full per-character bag data   (read-only source)
##   DaseekiBagsSets    — 1.x settings + custom rules       (read-only source)
##   DaseekiBagsMesh    — 1.x cross-account summaries       (read-only source)
##
core.lua
store.lua
# locks.lua: SORT LOCKS — the per-slot "never move this" marks and the LOCK CONFIG MODE
# the owner enters by RIGHT-CLICKING the sort glyph. 1.x carried this feature (sortButton
# OnLocking -> item PostClick/UpdateIgnored -> sorting GetSpaces); 2.0 had lost it.
# PURE: no WoW API at all, so the mode state machine and the 1.x lock import are fully
# harness-driven. Loads right after store.lua because it is a SETTINGS surface — locks
# nest inside the already-declared DaseekiBags2DB, so NO new SavedVariable is introduced
# (identity gate satisfied). Every consumer (sort.lua, ui_items.lua, ui_frame.lua,
# migrate_settings.lua) resolves ns.Locks at CALL time, so its position relative to
# them is free.
locks.lua
# nexus.lua: the SOFT-REQUIRED Daseeki-Nexus Inventory bridge. Bags sources its SUMMARY
# owners (the money tooltip's "Other Accounts" section and the cross-character tooltip
# counts) from the Nexus owners graph when that module is present and populated, and
# defers migrate.lua's DaseekiBagsMesh import to it. ZERO hard dependency: every call is
# type-guarded and falls back to Bags' own store, so the addon is unchanged without
# Nexus. Daseeki-Nexus is on the OptionalDeps line above for LOAD ORDER only -- so its
# SavedVariables are attached before our ADDON_LOADED runs Store.Init -> Migrate.Migrate
# and the deferral decision can see them. We never declare its globals here: declaring
# another addon's SavedVariables would make both addons write the same file.
# Loads after store.lua (it builds store-shaped owner records); every consumer resolves
# ns.Nexus at call time, so its position relative to them is free.
nexus.lua
capture.lua
migrate.lua
# migrate_settings.lua: the 1.x SETTINGS + custom-rules pass (audit NW-1), run at
# the same migration moment as migrate.lua's owner pass (migrate.Migrate calls it).
# Separate file because it converts DaseekiBagsSets, a different source with a
# different marker, and it consumes the search grammar at CALL time (not load time),
# so its position here is free.
migrate_settings.lua
# --- W2 (UI) --- loads after Daseeki-Core (Dependencies) so DaseekiUI exists ---
borders.lua
ui_items.lua
# parity.lua: the CELL-PARITY GATE. A line-cited transcription of what Daseeki-Bags 1.x
# draws in one bag cell, kept next to what 2.0 draws, with an assertion on every row. It
# exists to stop the "the grid still reads cluttered" loop becoming a fourth round of
# guessing: any per-cell element that drifts from the 1.x model turns the suite red and
# names the row. Pure Lua, no frames; loads after ui_items so both models are resolvable.
# Its 1.x line citations point at the source tree this cutover deleted: read them against
# tag v1.1.5 (or branch main), where those files are preserved verbatim.
parity.lua
ui_frame.lua
ui_owner.lua
ui_bank.lua
# --- W4 (search / sort / options) --- pure engines + DaseekiUI settings page ---
search.lua
# rules2 (W4.5): simplified custom-categories engine (a category = a saved search
# query; reuses the search.lua matcher). Consumed by ui_frame (combined-view category
# SECTIONS) and options.lua (the editor). MUST load — without it ns.Rules is nil, so
# Frame.CategoriesEnabled() is false and the combined view falls back to a flat grid
# with zero section headers (was the missing-categories beta defect). After search.lua
# (its matcher), before options.lua (the editor consumes ns.Rules).
rules2.lua
sort.lua
ui_find.lua
options.lua
features.lua
#
# Bindings.xml is deliberately NOT listed here. The client auto-discovers
# Bindings.xml at the addon root and hands it to the KEYBINDINGS parser. Listing
# it as a .toc file entry ALSO feeds it to the generic UI-XML parser, which does
# not know the <Binding> element -- that is what produced the LUA_WARNINGs the
# owner saw on every load ("Unrecognized XML: Binding", "Unrecognized XML
# attribute: header/category"). No shipping Era addon lists Bindings.xml in its
# .toc, and 1.x never did either. Do not re-add it.
#
# Corollary: because discovery is per FOLDER and by file name, this .toc cannot
# suppress Bindings.xml either. After the cutover there is exactly ONE Daseeki
# folder shipping it, so the "attempted to be loaded more than once" warning the
# side-by-side beta produced is gone by construction. If it ever returns, a second
# folder declaring our action names is installed -- an install-topology defect (a
# stray beta junction, a leftover Daseeki-Bags2 folder), never a reason to rename
# the actions: renaming is what orphans the owner's keys.
